update lr_explanation_choice_notes t set body=v.nb from (values
('910ca695-c885-4ed5-9bb8-2c4c9e249e5e'::uuid,'980807a53b1c94b0f48190d544225874','This combines the two claims Ashley attacks. Paula says there probably are intelligent beings on other planets and that they would "inevitably" develop technologies "such as radio communication." So Paula agrees it is probable that such beings exist and have radio.

Ashley disagrees. If our technology is a unique outgrowth of human physiology, cognitive makeup, and environment, there''s no reason to expect aliens to have radio. That''s exactly why Ashley calls the radio-telescope search a waste.

The move to repeat: find the claim the second speaker actually attacks, then pick the answer that restates it. Here that''s radio, not alien life itself.'),
('3d1872e8-5096-4a7b-ae9d-e8b6ff0b126e'::uuid,'675189f3a3d47dc6d13dd8ad88c2a14b','Paula says nothing about alien legal or political systems, so there''s no Agree position. Ashley raises them only as a comparison: assuming aliens share our physics is as unjustified as assuming they share our laws. A disagreement needs both speakers on record, and Paula is silent here.'),
('c08c751b-64fc-4535-acc4-b89cc0ce9b41'::uuid,'accd0a5d7d096c5eca0e53617ca03d46','Ashley agrees: technology is an "outgrowth" of our cognitive makeup. But can we find a Disagree position from Paula? No. Paula''s claim is about an outcome, that aliens would reach radio. Paula could grant that cognitive makeup influences technology and still think any intelligent species would get to radio anyway.'),
('59486f59-4475-4f97-8488-483d043fdbb5'::uuid,'086ee8a24f7f0072c378f9c74b5704fc','This is the tempting one, because Paula clearly agrees: there "probably are intelligent beings on other planets." Now look for Ashley''s Disagree position. Ashley attacks the method, radio telescopes, and the claim that aliens would share our science. Nothing Ashley says denies that intelligent life exists. Ashley''s objection makes perfect sense even if the aliens are out there and simply never built radios. With no Disagree position, D fails.'),
('418051c8-0731-4c10-af35-1233f66a84c7'::uuid,'dd9d9997b1b28646b846ff5d031f82b4','Paula never says scientists should spend more. Paula expects the telescopes to detect signs of aliens, which is a prediction, not a call for more funding, so there''s no Agree position. Ashley''s side is shaky too: calling a radio search "a waste of time and money" rejects one method, and Ashley could still back other ways of searching. Neither speaker is clearly on record.'),
('604339a1-b41d-4bc1-a2ec-5548d1327a84'::uuid,'313bd49d4287b88498bd3cb69ab2dfbc','Robin plainly accepts the nomadic/sedentary distinction. Does Kendall call it illicit? No. Kendall never says the categories are confused or improper, only that artifacts are a shaky way to sort cultures into them. Doubting a test is not the same as rejecting the categories it sorts into, so there''s no Disagree position.'),
('4523229d-3b31-4f8e-9b66-0b471170567c'::uuid,'872a850cb2fd67284c7838f5ac521ef7','Robin agrees. The first claim says artifacts can tell us whether a culture was nomadic or sedentary, and the second ties lasting artifacts to sedentary life. Put together, artifacts that were not made to last point toward nomadic.

Kendall disagrees. If materials largely determine what a people make, a settled culture with only flimsy materials would leave non-durable artifacts too. Then assuming "nomadic" from non-durable artifacts isn''t reasonable.

Notice that B isn''t Robin''s second claim word for word. It''s the other half of the test that Robin''s first claim commits Robin to, and it''s the half Kendall''s point actually breaks. Ask which claim the second speaker''s objection would make false, then match that claim.'),
('297d85c3-9a3c-4ce6-8cfc-b679b18393f1'::uuid,'f3c53bff609edebec1208102d3b30475','C asks whether evidence other than durability can establish a culture''s type conclusively. Robin never says durability is the only evidence, and Robin''s own claim is hedged with "likely." Kendall never mentions other evidence at all. With neither speaker on record, there''s nothing to disagree about.'),
('d83eee86-31b4-41da-8964-4fb4fd46f5a3'::uuid,'33662ddddafd89ee741e9170ad08b902','D brings in what "many archaeologists have thought" about the distinction''s importance. Neither speaker mentions other archaeologists or ranks how important the distinction is. Robin uses the distinction; Kendall questions one way of detecting it. Neither position is supported.'),
('51760e4b-3637-4f26-ab1b-f94b788805c2'::uuid,'ab1915666a580c6588b207b891a06df2','Robin agrees: artifacts can reveal whether a culture was nomadic or sedentary. Does Kendall disagree? Kendall says artifacts reflect the materials available, which is itself something artifacts reveal. Kendall doubts one inference, not the value of studying artifacts. Rejecting one use of the evidence isn''t saying the evidence reveals little, so there''s no Disagree position.'),
('5201a04c-8111-4353-93d9-b6599f6c1a52'::uuid,'aedcd5677b0cdd694452bc2effb6948a','Imagine two answers: twice the normal salt, or ten times. Either way, we still don''t know whether the rats had high blood pressure before the study. The amount of salt tells us how big the dose was, not whether the dose did anything.'),
('cb25f9bc-5c54-402b-b304-cef5fa0449e6'::uuid,'b8294f555e5d4ec249d3bb6fbffc0c53','Suppose the high blood pressure caused serious harm, or none at all. The conclusion is only that high-salt diets are linked to high blood pressure. What high blood pressure does afterward can''t tell us whether salt produced it.'),
('ea79abb1-e1f7-4376-b6f9-6235bf63eb65'::uuid,'e2b8ec31a54bf328f29b914afc454eea','How many wild colonies eat salty food tells us how common the diet is, not what it does. Whether the answer is 1 percent or 90 percent, this study''s evidence for a link is exactly as strong as before.'),
('64884a51-d794-4886-84ae-ed3ef63e7e32'::uuid,'837d07997b4dadba38fa76ebfbd041c1','This is the before-measurement the study is missing. Run both answers. If most of the colony already had abnormally high blood pressure, the after-numbers show nothing about salt and the conclusion falls apart. If almost none did, a jump to 75 percent points straight at the diet.

One question, two answers, opposite effects on the conclusion: that''s what a relevant Evaluate question does. Next time a study reports only results after a treatment, ask what the numbers were before it.'),
('834a2eeb-c931-4942-b72b-43979984981e'::uuid,'7f336a650ce87d814c2baeebbaa9a7ee','Yes or no, other rodent experiments say nothing about this colony. Even a yes wouldn''t tell us what those experiments found, and the conclusion is about rats anyway.'),
('d0e37fc7-a4b8-4a25-b6d4-ac1d0ed28730'::uuid,'38ebfc79eb9abf751795db7d5d98a4dc','This is the appeal-to-fear flaw: accept my claim, or something bad will happen to you. The herbalist never threatens anyone with consequences for skipping the juice. If anyone raised a fear, it was the doctors, and the herbalist is the one brushing that fear aside.'),
('e2ee319d-f723-40e0-9f20-44493b7aeb85'::uuid,'9c67ce0540b7fc3d8dda407410a41ba7','Check the premises against each other. Customers report better coordination; a few doctors say the herbs are potentially harmful. Both can be true at once: something can help your coordination and still carry a risk. No contradiction, so this flaw isn''t here.'),
('ae200272-4dfb-4865-a572-40b509bbc532'::uuid,'eb3e2b89749cfce9014c7c30f45f325c','The doctors claim the herbs are potentially harmful. The herbalist never says why that''s wrong. Instead, the herbalist points out that doctors want a monopoly, then concludes there''s no reason not to try the juice. A motive to make a claim doesn''t show the claim is false. A doctor with a monopoly to protect can still be right about a harmful herb.

That''s the Ad Hominem shape in answer-choice language: the argument goes after the "proponents" of a claim rather than the claim itself. When an argument answers a warning by talking about the people who gave it, look for this answer.'),
('6c54307e-858c-4e07-a8cc-87380a97345a'::uuid,'aff8cf0bfd81f87064661c69ed5b81ae','This describes circular reasoning, where the evidence already assumes the conclusion. The customer reports say coordination improved after drinking the juice. That wording doesn''t presuppose that the juice is safe or that there''s no reason to avoid it. The error is in how the herbalist handles the warning, not in how the evidence is phrased.'),
('78949977-e5e9-4cb9-8dab-2783c28b1b08'::uuid,'05f36bf48adb12fc8836c5872cdc27a2','The hippocampus is just one of the regions where theta waves occur. The argument never needs the hippocampus to cause anything; its leap is from theta waves to creativity. Delete the hippocampus from the stimulus and the flaw is still there.'),
('56f30592-2656-4219-9a42-0e7a63f26ae9'::uuid,'84066da687a95d44b1ef28cb67fe8cd9','Beck never weighs consistency against accuracy. Beck cares only about accuracy and uses consistency as evidence for it. The flaw is in treating consistency as proof of accuracy, not in ranking one above the other.'),
('ad3ae8af-3794-44f9-9116-44793695fe2e'::uuid,'de59ab3876de53cc98f8ab9ed2dad0b8','Beck''s conclusion is only about the accuracy of the program''s estimates of municipal automotive use. If the program were terrible at every other task, these estimates could still be accurate; if it were perfect elsewhere, these could still be wrong. Other tasks don''t bear on the conclusion.'),
('f93fb7ff-ccd7-4eab-9d10-7ee0723453fd'::uuid,'afdecc8cdd59cb088a57fd34d8708ce9','This is the trap, because it uses the right words in the wrong direction. Beck''s argument needs consistent output to come only from accurate estimates. C says Beck assumes the opposite: that the output would stay consistent even if the estimates were inaccurate.

If Beck believed that, Beck''s own evidence would be useless, since consistency would then show nothing about accuracy. Consistent-but-inaccurate is the possibility Beck ignores, not something Beck takes for granted. That''s E.'),
('bb95918f-0553-4fd1-b61f-ff29aee09c6d'::uuid,'60d35056c5513c3629d52f00c0ef604f','Beck never judges the program''s overall value. The argument is only about whether its estimates are accurate. And even if accuracy were the only thing that mattered, the problem would remain: consistency still wouldn''t prove it.'),
('ab3c156d-0a6e-4b4d-b199-95b79f886e58'::uuid,'1b5fc370feca5283f32921187ff1fd9e','This names the scale that is always five pounds off. Beck treats consistency as enough for accuracy, and E points to the case that breaks that link: output that is consistent but inaccurate.

Use the stem''s wording as a test. A "fails to consider X" answer is right when X, if true, would weaken the argument. If the program can be consistent and still wrong, Beck''s evidence no longer shows the estimates are accurate. That weakens it directly.'),
('0f248727-4894-4753-9003-dc641bf61838'::uuid,'05a9cb7625d830c657de08d38bebb1fb','This is the reversal in plain words. The premise says predators generally have short OPs. The conclusion needs the other direction: a mammal with a short OP is probably a predator. Those come apart whenever plenty of non-predators also have short OPs. If most short-OP mammals were not predators, Meg''s short OP would be weak evidence of predation.

The move: when a premise says most X are Y, and the conclusion treats a Y as probably an X, look for the answer saying most Y might not be X.'),
('802e05ee-fea9-400e-9d9e-37363437f3e8'::uuid,'05ef6cfb9754a877b4c3dffc014360a0','The argument never needs attacking prey to be the only reason predators move their forelimbs fast. It needs a short OP to signal predation. Grant that predators also need speed for other things, and the gap is untouched: short-OP non-predators could still outnumber short-OP predators.'),
('1eff26af-34ed-4722-a4a0-d857e02c710d'::uuid,'6a71d7af9d6b26f58c652eb08f4724f5','Meg did have a short OP, so whether Meg could have been a predator without one doesn''t matter. C raises whether a short OP is necessary for predation. The argument''s problem runs the other way: it treats a short OP as enough to indicate predation.'),
('21687cc2-1a02-4190-842c-5b501d928818'::uuid,'768fcc711fc6c1ab70c7b0a3b76465ff','"Takes for granted" means ask whether the argument needs it. Negate it: suppose extinct mammals did have shorter OPs on average than living ones. That might make Meg''s short OP a bit less telling, but it doesn''t settle what the argument depends on: whether short-OP mammals were mostly predators. The argument never needs D, so D isn''t the flaw.'),
('c789ea93-0028-4030-a9e8-b04f45b49ab9'::uuid,'512ca44028398205320ed112bd87abfb','E is about what a longer OP is good for. Meg had a short one. Whatever advantage long OPs give, it tells us nothing about whether short-OP mammals are mostly predators. To hurt the argument, we''d need non-predators that also have short OPs, and E never gives us any.'),
('bcc78acd-7f8d-404a-a93f-a667edb16350'::uuid,'43fd4b6373e089e47d076f4e088cb172','This describes the correlation flaw: two things often show up together, so they must be connected. The columnist never points to two things occurring together. The evidence is one attitude (accepting a risk beforehand), used to doubt a different attitude (resentment afterward).'),
('6bcbd390-1bdb-4adc-8476-7ff19ecc5f3a'::uuid,'7d8ac678d2d39e066a366a396d322dc1','The columnist never relies on subjects judging the scientific value of placebos. The forms only show what risk subjects accept, and the conclusion is about how they feel. Give every subject a pharmacology degree and the gap between signing the form and living with the outcome is still there.'),
('a450dc87-4178-4eba-acc1-6f6b5b6ab32f'::uuid,'c3957a9d32ec86b95880b20504626ecf','The forms come from the very people the ethicists are talking about, test subjects, and "almost all" of them accept the risk. Nothing suggests a skewed slice. Even a perfect sample would still measure the wrong moment: the attitude before the outcome rather than after it.'),
('db4f9adf-253b-4dc4-b2e4-24b58a5bfe5e'::uuid,'49ca8dcc53bb166c9dec156d32e9ddbb','This is the timing gap. The forms capture attitudes before the test; resentment comes once the substance turns out to be ineffective. For the forms to cast doubt on resentment, the columnist has to assume attitudes don''t change in between.

Negate it: suppose half or more of the subjects do change their attitudes once they learn their substance was ineffective. Then the forms tell us little about later resentment, and the argument falls apart. That''s what a "takes for granted" answer should name: an assumption whose negation breaks the argument.

For next time: when evidence about one moment is used to settle a question about a later moment, check whether the argument assumes nothing changed in between.'),
('97638749-e69c-4650-892e-349370a7db9b'::uuid,'4b4ed2f803b670b72bb5927643e95aed','The conclusion isn''t moral. It doesn''t say subjects should or shouldn''t resent placebos; it doubts that many of them do. That''s a factual claim about how people feel, supported by factual evidence about what they signed. There''s no jump from facts to a moral verdict.')
) v(id,ob,nb) where t.id=v.id and md5(t.body)=v.ob returning t.id;